Hmm.. I'm not sure I did understand what you mean, but I think towers
etc could be implemented more intelligently:
(This is a sort of castle.)
       _____________
      /            /  (level x-2)
     /____________/
       _____________
      /            /  (level x-1)
     /____________/
        ________________________________   
       /                               /
      /                               /     (level x)
     /_______________________________/
This whole thing is a SINGLE LEVEL castle. When you climb down from above,
you end in level x. Same from below. If you want to level x-2 you have to climb
this tower, etc. from x -> x-1 -> x-2. If you teleport out of the tower
on levels x-1 or x-2 you fall on level x. Levels x-1 and x-2 are hidden
from the level teleporter in such a way that you can't teleport into them.
I.e. the level just above this castle is, say, 5. The castle is then
on level 6 and level 7 is just below it.
Why do I think this is a great idea? Because I'm getting bored with having
all the levels looking about the same i.e. same sized rectangles.

